common Git problems in DevOps and simple solutions 

1. Problem: Merge Conflicts
What happens: When two people change the same part of a file, Git can’t automatically combine the changes.
 Solution:
Communicate with your team to avoid working on the same files at the same time.
Use git pull often to keep your branch updated.
Resolve conflicts manually by editing the conflicting files, then commit the changes.

2. Problem: Accidentally Committing Sensitive Data
What happens: You accidentally commit passwords or secret keys in your code.
 Solution:
Use .gitignore to prevent sensitive files from being committed.
If already committed, use tools like git filter-branch or BFG Repo-Cleaner to remove sensitive data from history.
Rotate/change the leaked secrets immediately.

3. Problem: Large Files Making Repo Slow
What happens: Big files slow down cloning and pushing.
 Solution:
Use Git Large File Storage (Git LFS) to manage large files outside the main repo.
Avoid committing large binaries if possible.

4. Problem: Forgot to Pull Before Pushing
What happens: You try to push your changes but get an error because your local branch is behind remote.
 Solution:
Always run git pull before git push to sync your local branch with the remote.
If there are conflicts, resolve them before pushing.

5. Problem: Deleted a Branch by Mistake
What happens: You accidentally delete a branch that has important work.
 Solution:
You can recover deleted branches using git reflog and git checkout <commit-hash> to restore it.

6. Problem: Wrong Commit Message or Commit Made to Wrong Branch
What happens: You commit with a wrong message or on the wrong branch.
 Solution:
Use git commit --amend to fix the last commit message.
Use git cherry-pick or git rebase to move commits to the correct branch.

7. Problem: Slow Cloning in Big Repos
What happens: Cloning takes a long time for big repositories.
 Solution:
Use shallow clone with git clone --depth=1 to clone only the latest commits.
